  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 PM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread clouds and onshore flow keep temps down \nthis afternoon. Showers and a few storms remain possible, mainly in \nnortheastern NC this afternoon. \n\nHigh pressure is building into the region from the north this \nafternoon. The slow-moving front has continued to drift south today \nand is currently in the vicinity of the Albemarle Sound and northern \nOBX. Post-frontal NE winds off the water and low stratus clouds have \nkept temperatures in the upper 70s to the low/mid 80s across the \nregion this afternoon. A few showers have developed across the \nsouthern half of the area but have been very short-lived. The deeper \nmoisture and higher potential for showers and storms continues to be \nfocused near and south of the VA/NC border and especially near the \nAlbemarle Sound. Locally heavy rainfall is possible adjacent to the \nAlbemarle Sound through early evening but the latest guidance \nlargely keeps the heavier precip south of the area. PoPs decrease \nrapidly toward sunset. Areas of fog and low stratus are possible \ntonight with low temps mainly in the mid and upper 60s.\n\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot conditions prevail during the early to middle \nportion of next week with increasing chances for showers and storms \nby late week. \n\nHigh pressure centers offshore Sunday, forcing the remnants of \nthe boundary well south of the local area. Outside of an \nisolated shower or storm, mostly dry weather and partly sunny \nskies are expected across the area. Highs on Sunday warm into \nthe upper 80s to around 90.\n\nA strong 500mb upper level ridge expands eastward over the Ohio \nValley and into the Mid-Atlantic early next week. Warm advection and \ndeep mixing will allow for temperatures to rise into the low to mid \n90s on Monday. The ridge peaks in strength Tuesday into Wednesday \nbefore flattening later in the weak. Tuesday through Thursday will \nsee high temperatures likely in the mid to even upper 90s for many \ninland locations. Max heat indices through the period should \ngenerally average 100-104 due to deep mixing keeping dewpoints in \nthe upper 60s to lower 70s. However, if dewpoints overperform, a \nHeat Advisory may be needed across portions of southeast Virginia \nand northeast North Carolina. \n\nConvective chances remain capped near climatological minimums \n(15-20% or less) through Wednesday due to strong mid level \nsubsidence. A broad upper trough and associated cold front \napproach from the Great Lakes Thursday into Friday, bringing the\nnext meaningful opportunity for showers and thunderstorms along\nwith more seasonable temperatures. AI/machine learning tools \ncontinue to hint at the possibility for severe weather Thursday \ninto Friday as the front approaches the area.",
